mcprack for Debian
===================

## Installation

Run the package installer, which will prompt for configuration interactively:

    sudo dpkg -i mcprack_*.deb

The installer will ask for:
- Database engine (sqlite3, PostgreSQL, or MySQL) and connection details,
  via the standard `dbconfig-common` prompts. sqlite3 is the default and
  needs no server; choosing pgsql/mysql has dbconfig-common create the
  database and role for you. Re-run `dpkg-reconfigure mcprack` later to
  change engine/connection details.
- Vaultwarden (Bitwarden) server URL, client ID, client secret, master password
- Whether to enable LDAP authentication (optional, disabled by default)
- If LDAP enabled: server URL, base DN, bind DN, bind password

All configuration is saved to `/etc/mcprack/env`. Database migrations
(`flask db upgrade`, via Alembic) run automatically on every install and
upgrade. If no admin account exists yet, an `admin` account with a randomly
generated password is created automatically — the password is written to
`/etc/mcprack/admin-credentials` (root-only, mode 0600) and printed once
during an interactive install.

## Manual Configuration (if not using interactive install)

Configuration lives in `/etc/mcprack/env`. Required variables:

    SECRET_KEY=<random-string>
    SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI=sqlite:////var/lib/mcprack/mcprack.db
    # or postgresql+psycopg2://... / mysql+pymysql://... (install python3-psycopg2
    # or python3-pymysql respectively — see Suggests in debian/control)
    #
    # Normally you don't set this by hand — `dpkg-reconfigure mcprack` drives
    # dbconfig-common to pick the engine and (re)write this value.

### Demo mode (optional, off by default)

    DEMO_MODE=true

For a public-facing instance: blocks registering/editing/deleting MCP servers
under Admin -> Servers (a server's `command` runs as-is whenever any user
connects to it, so this isn't something a public admin account should be able
to change), while catalog browsing/selection/config download keep working
normally. Restart the service after changing this.

### Exposing mcprack to the public internet

By default mcprack is intranet-oriented. To run it as an internet-facing
service, in addition to putting a TLS-terminating reverse proxy in front of
it (see below), review and set as needed:

    SECRET_KEY=<random-string>            # required — see below
    PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://mcprack.example.com   # set it: reset links, OAuth metadata, accepted Host
    SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=true            # default; needs the reverse proxy below
    GUNICORN_BIND=127.0.0.1:8912          # default; loopback only — see below
    LOGIN_MAX_FAILURES=5                  # default; per account AND address (shared by all workers)
    LOGIN_ACCOUNT_MAX_FAILURES=50         # default; per account over all addresses
    LOGIN_IP_MAX_FAILURES=30              # default; per address
    PASSWORD_RESET_RATE_LIMIT=5 per minute;20 per hour   # default; /forgot-password + /reset-password
    OAUTH_TOKEN_RATE_LIMIT=30 per minute  # default; /oauth/token, /oauth/revoke
    OAUTH_REGISTER_RATE_LIMIT=10 per minute;100 per hour   # default; /oauth/register (per IPv6 /64)
    MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH=2097152            # default 2 MiB request body cap
    STRICT_SERVER_PERMISSIONS=true        # default; a user sees only the servers an admin granted
    PROXY_TOKEN_MAX_AGE_SECONDS=7776000   # fresh installs: MCP proxy URLs expire after 90 days

mcprack refuses to start if `SECRET_KEY` is missing, a placeholder
(`change-me…`, `dev-…`, `secret`, …), shorter than 32 characters or has too
little variety — it signs Flask sessions, the per-user MCP proxy URLs and
password-reset links and protects the locally encrypted secrets, so treat it
like a password. To **rotate** it without logging everybody out, breaking
every client configuration and losing the local secret store, keep the old
value in `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS=<old1>,<old2>` (comma separated; each entry must
be as strong as a SECRET_KEY) until nothing signed with it is left.

`STRICT_SERVER_PERMISSIONS` is now **true by default**: a user with no
explicit grant sees no server at all, which is what an internet-facing
instance needs (any directory account is provisioned on its first LDAP login).
On upgrade a data migration (`materialize_server_permissions`) gives every
*existing* user an explicit grant for each server enabled at that moment, so
nobody loses access. Servers created later, and new users, need a grant
(Admin → Users, or apply a template). On a trusted intranet where everybody
should see everything, set `STRICT_SERVER_PERMISSIONS=false`.

`GUNICORN_BIND` is where gunicorn listens; it defaults to `127.0.0.1:8912`,
i.e. loopback only, so gunicorn cannot be reached directly from the network
and TLS cannot be bypassed — no firewall rule needed for that. A reverse
proxy on the same host reaches it as usual. Two cases need it changed in
`/etc/mcprack/env` (then `systemctl restart mcprack`):

  * the reverse proxy runs on a *different* host, or
  * you deliberately serve plain HTTP with no reverse proxy at all (a
    private intranet install) — then set `GUNICORN_BIND=0.0.0.0:8912`.

In both of those cases the port is exposed to the network, so restrict it to
whatever is actually allowed to reach it, e.g. for a proxy on another host:

    nft add rule inet filter input tcp dport 8912 ip saddr != <proxy-ip> drop

`SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=true` (the default) means the browser will not send
the session cookie back except over HTTPS, so mcprack must sit behind a
TLS-terminating reverse proxy that sets `X-Forwarded-Proto: https` — a
sample Apache vhost covering TLS, HSTS, and the required forwarded headers
is at `/usr/share/doc/mcprack/apache-mcprack.conf` (source:
`debian/apache-mcprack.conf`). If you're intentionally running plain HTTP
with no reverse proxy (e.g. a private intranet install), set
`SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=false` instead.

The `?username=&password=` query-string prefill on `/login` (used by the
public demo link) only takes effect when `DEMO_MODE=true` — it's ignored
otherwise, so it can't leak a real password into browser history or access
logs.

See also the "Security considerations for public-internet deployments"
section in the main README for known limitations (path-token revocation,
admin-account compromise) that aren't fully closed by configuration alone.
Internet-facing installs should prefer `OAUTH_ENABLED=true` (below) so
proxy credentials are revocable Bearer tokens instead of signed URL path
secrets.

### Hardening in this release: upgrade notes and internet checklist

New behaviour you should know about (details: `docs/AUDIT-2026-11.md`):

* **Password policy**: at least 12 characters (`PASSWORD_MIN_LENGTH`), no empty,
  common or username-based passwords, for every way a password is set. Existing
  passwords are untouched; an account that holds a blank password can no longer
  log in (reset it with `mcprack user passwd`).
* **Login throttling** is stored in the database, so it holds across workers and
  restarts; failed logins no longer put the typed name into the audit log.
  Locked-out users see HTTP 429 with `Retry-After`.
* **Logout** (the menu button) ends the account's sessions everywhere; changing
  the password does too. A copy of a session cookie no longer outlives either.
* **Taking access away**: disabling or deleting a user stops their running MCP
  servers and the proxy URL stops working. `mcprack user revoke-tokens NAME`
  (or Admin → Users, or `POST /api/v1/admin/users/{id}/revoke-tokens`)
  invalidates every proxy URL, API token and OAuth token of a user; they must
  download a new configuration. Tokens issued before this release keep working
  until revoked.
* **Per-user overrides**: a user may only set variables the server declares,
  plus credential-looking names (`MY_API_TOKEN`); process-altering names
  (`PATH`, `LD_*`, `NODE_OPTIONS`, proxy and CA settings …) are never accepted.
  `OVERRIDE_UNDECLARED_KEYS=any` allows other free-form names (the denylist still
  applies). If a user re-points an endpoint variable (`…_URL`, `…_HOST`,
  `…_INSTANCE`) the admin's *shared* secrets are not passed along with it.
* **MCP server processes** inherit only `PATH`, `LANG`, `LC_*`, `HOME`, proxy and
  CA-bundle settings — none of mcprack's own secrets. Add names with
  `MCP_CHILD_ENV_ALLOW=NAME1,NAME2` (secrets can never be added). Their
  resolved credentials now live in `/run/mcprack` (tmpfs, 0600, removed when the
  process is reaped or the service stops), not in `/var/lib/mcprack`.
* **Package installer**: registry packages only (no URLs/paths/VCS), pinned to an
  exact version by default (`INSTALL_REQUIRE_PINNED=false` to allow ranges); pip
  installs wheels only (`INSTALL_ALLOW_SOURCE_BUILDS=true` to allow sdists); npm
  never runs lifecycle scripts.
* **LDAP**: use `ldaps://` or `LDAP_STARTTLS=true`; the directory certificate is
  verified (`LDAP_CA_FILE`, `LDAP_TLS_VALIDATE`). Plain `ldap://` still works
  and is flagged in the log. `LDAP_REQUIRED_GROUP=<group DN>` restricts login
  to members of a group.
* **OAuth** (only with `OAUTH_ENABLED=true`): dynamic client registration accepts
  loopback redirects, `https://` redirects to `OAUTH_ALLOWED_REDIRECT_HOSTS`
  (default `claude.ai,claude.com`; `*.example.com` allowed) and custom schemes
  listed in `OAUTH_ALLOWED_REDIRECT_PREFIXES`; the consent page shows where the
  code goes; tokens are stored hashed; refresh tokens are really issued and last
  `OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRES`. Registration is capped (`OAUTH_MAX_CLIENTS`);
  a coordinated flood from many addresses can still fill it for
  `OAUTH_CLIENT_MIN_AGE_SECONDS` — restrict `/oauth/register` at the proxy if
  that matters to you.
* **Public demo (`DEMO_MODE=true`)**: all routes that register servers, change
  accounts or passwords, or move secrets are refused, including through the REST
  API. Overrides there accept only declared variable names.
* **systemd**: gunicorn uses threads (`GUNICORN_THREADS`, default 4) so one slow
  MCP server cannot occupy a whole worker; `RuntimeDirectory=mcprack` holds the
  volatile state.

Checklist before exposing an instance to the internet:

1. `SECRET_KEY` strong; `PUBLIC_BASE_URL` set (also restricts the accepted Host);
   TLS terminated by a reverse proxy; `GUNICORN_BIND` left on loopback.
2. The Apache vhost from `apache-mcprack.conf`: dead-end default vhost, client
   `X-Forwarded-*` headers dropped, request size limits, and — recommended —
   `/admin` and `/api/v1/admin` restricted to your network, because anyone who
   can register a server can run commands as the service user.
3. Grant servers explicitly (`STRICT_SERVER_PERMISSIONS`), and prefer OAuth or
   short-lived proxy URLs (`PROXY_TOKEN_MAX_AGE_SECONDS`).
4. Keep outbound traffic of the `mcprack` user in check — MCP servers are
   arbitrary programs. Example (nftables), blocking cloud metadata and loopback
   backends other than the proxy ports:

       table inet mcprack_egress {
         chain output {
           type filter hook output priority 0; policy accept;
           meta skuid mcprack ip daddr 169.254.0.0/16 reject
           meta skuid mcprack ip6 daddr fe80::/10 reject
           # add: meta skuid mcprack ip daddr { <networks MCP servers must NOT reach> } reject
         }
       }

5. fail2ban on the **Apache** access log (gunicorn only ever sees 127.0.0.1).
   A failed login answers 200 (the form again) or 429; a good one 302:

       # /etc/fail2ban/filter.d/mcprack-login.conf
       [Definition]
       failregex = ^<HOST> \S+ \S+ \[[^\]]+\] "POST /(login|forgot-password|oauth/register)\S* HTTP/[0-9.]+" (200|429) 
       # /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/mcprack.conf
       [mcprack-login]
       enabled  = true
       filter   = mcprack-login
       logpath  = /var/log/apache2/mcprack-access.log
       maxretry = 10
       findtime = 10m
       bantime  = 1h

6. Verify with `curl -H 'Host: x.invalid' https://<server>/login` (must not reach
   mcprack) and, after setting `PUBLIC_BASE_URL`, a password-reset e-mail link.

### Authentication (optional, LDAP disabled by default)

    LDAP_ENABLED=false
    # To enable LDAP:
    LDAP_ENABLED=true
    LDAP_SERVER=ldap://10.11.25.3:389
    LDAP_BASE_DN=dc=spojent,dc=cz
    LDAP_USER_OU=ou=Users,dc=spojent,dc=cz
    LDAP_USER_FILTER=(sAMAccountName=%(user)s)
    LDAP_BIND_DN=cn=admin,dc=spojent,dc=cz
    LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD=...

### Vaultwarden (Bitwarden) Credentials

    BW_SERVER=https://vaultwarden-dev.proxy.spojenet.cz
    BW_CLIENTID=...
    BW_CLIENTSECRET=...
    BW_PASSWORD=...
    BITWARDENCLI_APPDATA_DIR=/var/lib/mcprack/.bw

Get `BW_CLIENTID`/`BW_CLIENTSECRET` from the Vaultwarden web vault: Account
Settings → Security → Keys (API Key). `BW_PASSWORD` is that same account's
master password. After setting these, log in to mcprack as an admin and open
"Vaultwarden diagnostics" in the nav bar (`/admin/vaultwarden/wizard`) — it
checks the `bw` binary, `BW_SERVER` reachability, the API key, and the master
password one at a time and tells you exactly which one is still wrong,
instead of a raw "bw login failed" error. Re-run it after every edit to
`/etc/mcprack/env`, then restart `mcprack.service` so new values are loaded.

### Manual Setup Steps (if needed)

Migrations and the initial admin account are created automatically by
`postinst` — see above. If you need to redo either by hand (e.g. after
restoring a database dump):

    # Apply migrations
    su -s /bin/sh mcprack -c 'cd /usr/lib/mcprack && set -a; . /etc/mcprack/env; set +a; mcprack db upgrade'

    # Create another admin account
    su -s /bin/sh mcprack -c 'cd /usr/lib/mcprack && set -a; . /etc/mcprack/env; set +a; mcprack create-admin'

Then start the service:

    systemctl enable --now mcprack.service

### Command-line administration (user / server / secret)

Beyond `create-admin`, the `mcprack` binary also forwards `user`,
`server`, and `secret` subcommands for managing accounts, catalog
servers, and their credential values without the web UI, e.g.:

    su -s /bin/sh mcprack -c 'cd /usr/lib/mcprack && set -a; . /etc/mcprack/env; set +a; mcprack user create --username alice --admin'
    su -s /bin/sh mcprack -c 'cd /usr/lib/mcprack && set -a; . /etc/mcprack/env; set +a; mcprack server list'

See the "Command-line administration" section in the main README, or
`man mcprack`, for the full command reference.


## Troubleshooting

### "Permission denied" reading /etc/mcprack/env during postinst

Packages built before 0.2.13 created `/etc/mcprack/env` mode `0600`
(owner-only), so despite the `root:mcprack` group ownership the `mcprack`
system user couldn't actually read it. Postinst's own `flask db upgrade`
and `create-admin` steps run as that user, so they failed silently with:

    sh: 1: .: cannot open /etc/mcprack/env: Permission denied
    ⚠  Database migration failed — check /etc/mcprack/env and run 'flask db upgrade' manually
    ⚠  Failed to create initial admin account — create one manually with 'flask create-admin'

0.2.14 fixes this going forward (permissions are corrected unconditionally
on every postinst run, not just on first install), but an install that hit
this under an older package needs a one-time manual fix:

    sudo chmod 0640 /etc/mcprack/env
    sudo su -s /bin/sh mcprack -c "cd /usr/lib/mcprack && set -a; . /etc/mcprack/env; set +a; mcprack db upgrade"
    sudo su -s /bin/sh mcprack -c "cd /usr/lib/mcprack && set -a; . /etc/mcprack/env; set +a; mcprack create-admin"

### "table already exists" from `flask db upgrade`

If the service ever started successfully *before* migrations ever ran
(e.g. while hitting the permission bug above, on a package older than
0.2.15), `db.create_all()`'s startup fallback already created every table
without recording an Alembic version. A later `flask db upgrade` then
tries to run the very first migration from scratch and fails:

    sqlalchemy.exc.OperationalError: (pymysql.err.OperationalError) (1050, "Table 'mcp_servers' already exists")

0.2.15 stamps Alembic to head right after that fallback runs, so this
shouldn't happen on fresh bootstraps going forward. If you're recovering
an install that already hit it, tell Alembic the schema is current instead
of trying to upgrade it:

    sudo su -s /bin/sh mcprack -c "cd /usr/lib/mcprack && set -a; . /etc/mcprack/env; set +a; mcprack db stamp head"

## MCP HTTP Proxy (how remote clients reach stdio servers)

Users always connect remotely — mcprack never expects its own web UI and a
user's MCP client to be on the same machine. So a stdio-implemented server
is never handed to a client as a raw local spawn command; the downloaded
config always points at a per-user proxy URL instead
(`/proxy/mcp/<token>/<server_id>` by default, or `/proxy/mcp/<server_id>`
when `OAUTH_ENABLED=true`, served by `mcprack.service` itself, no
separate proxy service to install or enable).

The first time a user's client actually connects to that URL, mcprack spawns
a dedicated `fastmcp` subprocess for that (user, server) pair on demand,
resolving that user's credentials (their own override if they've set one,
otherwise the admin's default) at that exact moment — not when the config
was downloaded. Each user gets their own isolated instance of a server, even
if several users have it selected at once. Idle instances (no traffic for 15
minutes) are cleaned up automatically; see Admin → Proxy instances for a live
list, and `python3-fastmcp` must be installed (see Recommends in
`debian/control`) for this to work. mcprack finds the binary itself —
`FASTMCP_BIN` if set, otherwise `PATH`, otherwise `/usr/bin/fastmcp` — and
reports a spawn failure naming the package when none of those is executable.
Set `FASTMCP_BIN` in `/etc/mcprack/env` only for a fastmcp outside the
system path (e.g. in a virtualenv).

Cold-spawn timing is tunable via two env vars (see `user_proxy.py`):

    MCP_PROXY_HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT=15   # seconds to wait for a fresh spawn to answer
    MCP_PROXY_LOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT=22   # seconds a concurrent request waits on the spawn lock

If proxy instances fail with "failed its startup handshake" on a slow host
even though the registered command is fine, the fresh interpreter + import
cost is likely just exceeding `MCP_PROXY_HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT` — raise it (and
`MCP_PROXY_LOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT` along with it, keeping both comfortably under
gunicorn's own 30s worker timeout in `debian/mcprack.service`).

### Optional MCP OAuth (off by default)

When enabled, downloaded configs use the stable `/proxy/mcp/<server_id>`
URL and MCP clients authenticate with `Authorization: Bearer` after the
standard MCP Authorization Spec discovery + OAuth dance. Path-token URLs
remain valid.

    OAUTH_ENABLED=true
    PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://mcprack.example.com
    # OAUTH_ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRES=3600
    # OAUTH_ALLOW_INSECURE_TRANSPORT=true   # plain-HTTP lab only

Requires `python3-authlib` (Depends) and HTTPS in production (authlib
refuses non-HTTPS authorize/token URLs unless
`OAUTH_ALLOW_INSECURE_TRANSPORT=true`). `PUBLIC_BASE_URL` is also used as
the OAuth issuer in discovery metadata — set it to the origin clients
actually reach. After enabling, restart `mcprack.service`; the
`add_oauth_tables` migration runs via the usual postinst `db upgrade`.

Endpoints served when enabled:

    GET  /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server
    GET  /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource
    GET  /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/proxy/mcp/<id>
    POST /oauth/register
    GET/POST /oauth/authorize
    POST /oauth/token
    POST /oauth/revoke
    *    /proxy/mcp/<server_id>     # Bearer: OAuth access token or ApiToken with proxy:access

See the main README "Optional MCP OAuth" section for the full client flow.


## OpenTelemetry (optional — packaging gap on Debian, read before enabling)

`OTEL_ENABLED=true` in `/etc/mcprack/env` turns on trace/metric export (see
README.md "Observability (OpenTelemetry)"). It is safe to enable even
without every dependency installed — `telemetry.py` catches any import
failure at startup, logs it, and falls back to fully disabled rather than
crashing the service.

As of Debian trixie, only two of the required Python packages are actually
available via `apt`:

    python3-opentelemetry-api
    python3-opentelemetry-sdk
    python3-opentelemetry-instrumentation   (base instrumentation library)

There is **no Debian package** (checked via `apt-cache search opentelemetry`
on trixie) for:

    opentelemetry-instrumentation-flask
    opentelemetry-instrumentation-sqlalchemy
    opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-http
    opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-grpc

Without these four, `OTEL_ENABLED=true` will safely no-op (nothing is
exported, no crash). To actually export anything, either:

- Package those four for Debian (see the `package-python-debian` workflow
  used for other VitexSoftware Python packaging), or
- Install them into a dedicated venv and point mcprack's systemd unit at
  that venv's Python instead of the system interpreter — this deviates
  from mcprack's normal "no virtualenv, system Python packages" packaging
  model (see main README "Packaging"), so treat it as a stopgap, not the
  long-term fix.

Check **Admin → OTEL Diagnostics** (`/admin/otel/wizard`) after enabling —
if `OTEL_ENABLED` shows as *disabled* there even though you set it to
`true` in `/etc/mcprack/env`, initialization failed (missing package) and
the page shows the exact import error under "Last initialization error".
The same error is also in `journalctl -u mcprack.service`.
